<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Edge-to-edge post-splash theme. Compose supplies all real theming; this exists so the
         window is dark from the very first frame (no white flash). -->
    <style name="Theme.LumoBrowser" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/lumo_backdrop</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowActivityTransitions">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowContentTransitions">true</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The launch theme: what the system paints between "the user tapped the icon" and "the first
        Compose frame is on screen".

        Before this existed the activity launched straight into `Theme.LumoBrowser`, whose only
        visible property is a flat `@color/lumo_backdrop`. So the user got an empty dark rectangle,
        and only then the in-app startup overlay faded its mark in on top of it — the "black screen
        before the splash animation" cold start this theme set out to remove. The platform draws the
        splash window from the moment the process is forked, so the brand mark is now on screen from
        the first frame the system is able to draw anything at all.

        The parent is the library's `Theme.SplashScreen`, which is the contract
        `SplashScreen.installSplashScreen()` needs: it resolves `postSplashScreenTheme` from the
        activity's current theme, and only a descendant of `Theme.SplashScreen` is guaranteed to
        declare one. On API 31+ it is also what attaches the platform attributes below to the real
        system splash window.

        `Theme.LumoBrowser` stays the post-splash theme, so the platform tears its splash window down
        onto exactly the window this app always had.

        See values-night/themes.xml for why the night copy repeats these literal values rather than
        flipping to a light backdrop.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.LumoBrowser.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <!-- The brand mark, not a second asset: this is the adaptive launcher icon's own foreground
             (the orbit ring around the luminous core). The adaptive icon is deliberately NOT
             referenced here — below API 31 an adaptive icon used as a splash icon is cropped and
             scaled unpredictably, and pointing at the foreground drawable is the documented
             workaround for that. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground</item>
        <!-- Same colour the Compose startup overlay paints as its own backdrop
             (`LumoThemeState.backgroundBase` is 0xFF06070E in the dark scheme, and this is
             0xFF06070E), so the system window and the overlay are one continuous surface rather than
             two dark rectangles that happen to be adjacent. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/lumo_backdrop</item>
        <!-- Length of the ICON's exit animation, not how long the splash stays up: that is decided by
             when the activity's first frame is ready (the library's own docs are explicit that this
             attribute has no impact on dwell time, and `setKeepOnScreenCondition` is the tool for
             that — deliberately not used here, because gating the first frame behind anything is the
             failure mode this app has already shipped once).

             Zero, so the launch window hands the screen over instead of animating it away. The value
             used to be 400 ms, and that was the wrong half of the hand-off to animate: the Compose
             overlay now draws this same mark, at this same place, on this same colour, from its very
             first frame (see StartupAnimation.kt), so a platform exit animation would fade the mark
             out and then the app would draw it again — the mark playing twice, which is the one thing
             the brief rules out. With no exit animation the swap is invisible by construction: a
             static mark on `@color/lumo_backdrop` is replaced by the same static mark on the same
             colour. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
        <!-- Applied by installSplashScreen() before setContent, which is what makes the activity
             come out the other side as the ordinary app theme. -->
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.LumoBrowser</item>
        <!-- The parent's `android:windowBackground` is part of its compat splash machinery. Below
             API 31 that drawable is the launch window the user actually stares at, so it is placed
             back on the same backdrop colour the Compose overlay will paint: an API 26–30 device
             gets a solid dark launch window with the mark centred in it, and the hand-off to the
             overlay is a continuation instead of a colour change. On API 31+ the platform attrs
             above win and this is only the window background the activity starts with. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/lumo_backdrop</item>
    </style>
</resources>
